ptured the city  in may  russian state tv filming this video a  sign of daily life operating in a new  normal under russian occupation but  ukraine's defense minister says nato  weapons will help his forces to launch a  counter-offensive to try and retake  parts of the black sea coast if those  weapons arrive more quickly if that is  done this would be feasible  but it needs to be done very very  quickly ukraine is also frustrated with  canada after ottawa bypassed sanctions  allowing a repaired turbine to be sent  from montreal to europe for use on the  russia-germany nordstrom one gas  pipeline we need to understand that  larger picture  and part of that larger picture is  that in a conflict  you need to make sacrifices ironically  it may be for nothing the pipeline was  shut down on monday for 10 days of  maintenance work there are fears russia  may keep it closed sacrificing the  massive income it gets from europe to  spite its western adversaries at this  point is not important for russia for  the revenues it is important because it  is a geostrategic tool eu countries have  paid russia an estimated 89 billion  dollars for fossil fuels since  february's invasion and there's no end  in sight redmond shannon global news  london  well we are getting a first look at the  deepest reaches of outer space ever  captured the first image from the  largest space telescope ever built has  just been unveiled it comes from nasa's  10 billion dollar james webb space  telescope  it's the deepest and sharpest infrared  image of the distant universe to date  four more images will be released  tomorrow and will include a planet from  outside our solar system  preparing for a painful visit with the  pope ahead reflections from residential  school survivors  the first nations communities of  muscules near edmonton will soon be in  the international spotlight when pope  francis begins his canadian visit the  pope plans to spend time with  residential school survivors at the site  of what was once one of the country's  largest institutions with thousands  expected to attend as heather urix west  explains the community is bracing for  emotional impact  with just two weeks until the pope  arrives in musculatus alberta the  community is working hard to prepare  for many this visit will be tough i'm at  peace and i'm really trying to be stay  strong and positive  trying to stay focused why he's coming  karen wildcat attended the ehrman skin  residential school for three years as a  child an experience she says that  upended her entire life as one of the  largest institutions of its kind in the  country the earmanskin residential  school operated from 1895 to 1975. those  who attended like bruce cutknife  remember it as a time full of trauma and  abuse the children were always crying in  the class  seeing a girl get beaten with a  yardstick and the teacher broke the  yardstick in half hitting her  took both hats of the yards to continue  you know hitting a child the pope is  expected to spend time with survivors of  this school and others like it and to  again apologize for the hand the church  had in this abuse we've heard a whole  range of emotions we've heard people who  are happy and  happy that he's coming and looking  forward to the apology and others who  are angry community leaders say they  know the visit will be painful 150  mental health workers will also attend  an historic but difficult moment of  acknowledgement and accountability that  survivors know will likely never come  again  heather urex west global news calgary  he was the murderer known as mom  next maurice boucher's long life of  violent crime  one of canada's most notorious criminals  died over the weekend maurice boucher  commonly known as mom boucher had been  battling cancer  the former hells angels boss was serving  a life sentence for the murders of two  prison guards mike armstrong reports  it's a time that looking back is almost  hard to believe this was maurice  mambouche at a court appearance for his  son boucher was the head of an elite  chapter of the hell's angels everyone  knew mambushi was a killer and he knew  everyone knew  he seemed to love it  quebec's biker war raged for eight years  more than 160 people were killed and it  made the man at the middle of it  infamous  bouchet led the nomads an elite chapter  of the hell's angels he got the nickname  mom because he was apparently like an  overbearing mother for a time he seemed  untouchable  he wasn't but when boucher was arrested  in 1997 he was already a household name  he wouldn't be behind bars for long and  his reputation was still growing  was put on trial for ordering the murder  of two prison guards the goal was to  destabilize the justice system  [Music]  he won the first round the jury in  bouche's original trial found him not  guilty  [Applause]  laughing wouldn't last  a second trial was ordered on appeal one  of the reasons jurors had been  intimidated bouche was found guilty the  second time around  he would spend the rest of his days in  prison but still plugged into organized  crime in fact still ordering murders in  2018 he had another 10 years tacked on  to a life sentence  now the reason boucher became what some  have called a folk here on quebec is  because most of the victims of the biker  war were criminals but not all  for journalists who covered those years  there are names of innocent victims that  are impossible to forget the prison  guards who were killed diane levine  the 11 year old boy playing in a park  killed when a jeep nearby was blown up  and eve albert the father of two gunned  down gassing up his car he was shot nine  times because he was mistaken for a  biker  if for a time boucher lived a life that  seemed to be above the law it didn't  last he spent his final two decades  locked up what did last is the pain he  caused  mike armstrong global news  montreal  and that is global national for this  monday night i'm tracy tong tonight'